```java
class TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode left, right;
    TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
}

// Manually build:
TreeNode root = new TreeNode(1);
root.left = new TreeNode(2);
root.right = new TreeNode(3);
root.left.left = new TreeNode(4);
// Tree:
//       1
//      / \
//     2   3
//    /
//   4
```

**Properties**:
- No ordering between left and right.
- Search must visit every node → O(n).
- Used when **structure matters** more than order.

**Use cases**:
- **Expression trees**: `(a + b) * c` → root `*`, left subtree `+(a,b)`, right `c`.
- **Decision trees**: each node is a question, left/right are answers.
- **File-system trees**: directories and files (though often N-ary, not binary).
- **Huffman coding tree**: variable-length codes for compression.
- **Heaps** (special case): complete binary tree with heap property; stored as array.
- **AST (abstract syntax tree)**: parsed code structure.
- **Game trees** (e.g., minimax for chess) — though typically N-ary.

**Operations**:
- **Insert/delete** are O(1) if you have the position; O(n) to find a position.
- **Search**: O(n) — traverse all nodes.
- **Traversal**: in-order, pre-order, post-order, level-order (BFS) — all O(n).

**Special structural variants**:
- **Full binary tree**: every node has 0 or 2 children.
- **Complete binary tree**: all levels full except possibly last, filled left-to-right.
- **Perfect binary tree**: all internal nodes have 2 children, all leaves at same depth.
- **Balanced binary tree**: heights of left and right subtrees differ by at most 1.

```java
class BST {
    TreeNode root;

    public void insert(int val) {
        root = insertRec(root, val);
    }

    private TreeNode insertRec(TreeNode node, int val) {
        if (node == null) return new TreeNode(val);
        if (val < node.val) node.left = insertRec(node.left, val);
        else if (val > node.val) node.right = insertRec(node.right, val);
        return node;
    }

    public boolean search(int val) {
        TreeNode cur = root;
        while (cur != null) {
            if (val == cur.val) return true;
            cur = val < cur.val ? cur.left : cur.right;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

**BST property**: for every node, all left descendants < node < all right descendants.

**In-order traversal yields sorted output** — the defining property of BSTs:
```java
void inorder(TreeNode node) {
    if (node == null) return;
    inorder(node.left);
    System.out.print(node.val + " ");   // visit in sorted order
    inorder(node.right);
}
// For BST built from {5, 3, 7, 1, 4}: prints 1 3 4 5 7
```

**Operations — average O(log n) for balanced trees**:
- Search: walk left/right based on comparison.
- Insert: find position, attach as leaf.
- Delete: three cases (leaf, one child, two children — replace with in-order successor).
- Min/Max: leftmost/rightmost node.
- Predecessor / successor: O(log n).
- Range query [a, b]: traverse only relevant subtrees, O(log n + k).

**Worst case O(n)**: degenerate tree (skewed). Happens when:
- Insertions are sorted: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 → right-only chain.
- Reverse-sorted insertions: 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 → left-only chain.
- Adversarial input pattern.

**Solution: self-balancing BSTs**.
